[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mPath: architectural.[0m A notifications subsystem is new structure, not a change to an existing flow — so this gets the
  full treatment: questions, approaches, a design you approve section by section, then a written spec before any code.

  First, what's actually here: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is 11 lines — a title, an [38;5;153m<h1>[39m, and an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. There is no task model,
  no storage, no server, no users, no build tooling. Nothing else in the repo.

  That matters because "notify users when tasks they care about change" presupposes three things this app doesn't have
  yet: [1mtasks[0m, [1musers[0m, and [1ma place where changes happen that someone else can observe[0m. I'm not going to guess those into
  existence — the guesses would determine the entire notifications design and would be expensive to undo.

  So the first fork, which drives everything after it:

  [1mThe fork:[0m is this a single-user app running in one browser, or a multi-user app with a server?

  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — "notification" means an in-page indicator or toast when something changes in the current
    session (or since the last visit, via localStorage). Cheap; no backend. Rules out: notifying anyone while they
    aren't looking at the page, and any cross-user "someone else changed your task" story. Genuinely hard to retrofit
    into multi-user later.
  - [1mMulti-user with a server[0m — tasks live server-side, changes are events, notifications are per-user records with read
    state and a delivery channel. This is the design that matches your sentence literally. Costs: you need auth,
    persistence, and an API before notifications are even meaningful. Those are separate subsystems and would each need
    their own spec.
  - [1mDesign the notifications layer against a task model that doesn't exist yet[0m — define the event contract and
    notification store now, stub the task source. Keeps notifications honest and testable, defers the backend. Cost:
    you're building against an interface nobody has validated, so some rework is likely when the real task system lands.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me which of these you're actually heading toward before I design anything. If the honest
  answer is "this is an empty repo and I want the whole thing," then notifications is the wrong first sub-project —
  tasks and users come first, and notifications gets its own spec afterward.

